<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="other" xml:lang="EN"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Emerg Infect Dis</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">EID</journal-id><journal-title>Emerging Infectious Diseases</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1080-6040</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1080-6059</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2609869</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">08-0000</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.3201/eid1410.080000</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>News and Notes</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>etymologia: <italic>Coxiella</italic> [kok&#x02032;&#x02032; se-el&#x02032; &#x004d9;] <italic>burnetii</italic></article-title></title-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>10</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><volume>14</volume><issue>10</issue><fpage>1566</fpage><lpage>1566</lpage></article-meta></front><body><p>Etiologic agent of Q fever, named after American bacteriologist Herald Rea Cox and Australian physician Frank MacFarlane Burnet, who both independently isolated the bacterium in the 1930s. <italic>C. burnetii</italic> belongs in the family <italic>Coxiellaceae</italic>, which consists of gram-negative rods without flagella or a capsule. The bacteria occur in ticks and various vertebrates, including humans. </p></body><back><fn-group><fn id="FN1"><p>Sources: Dorland&#x02019;s illustrated medical dictionary, 31st edition. Philadelphia: Saunders; 2007; American Veterinary Medical Association. Q fever backgrounder. Available from <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.avma.org/public_health/biosecurity/qfever_bgnd.asp">http://www.avma.org/public_health/biosecurity/qfever_bgnd.asp</ext-link></p></fn></fn-group></back></article>